WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.580
std::unique_ptr is often the right default when dynamic ownership is necessary.

2
00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:11.000
It says that exactly one owner is responsible for destroying the object.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:17.340
That simplicity is valuable during security review because ownership transfer is visible through move semantics.

4
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:22.320
A copied unique_ptr is not allowed; a moved one makes the handoff explicit.

5
00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.760
Factory functions are a common pattern.

6
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:31.100
A factory can build an object and return std::unique_ptr , making the caller's ownership clear.

7
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:40.240
Member fields can also use unique_ptr when an object owns a subobject whose lifetime should end with the containing object.

8
00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:45.140
This avoids raw owning pointers and makes destruction predictable through RAII.

9
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.820
Safe use still requires discipline.

10
00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:57.680
Passing ptr.get() can be appropriate for short borrowed access, but the callee must not delete or store that raw pointer unless a separate contract guarantees lifetime.

11
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:03.540
Calling release() is review-sensitive because it gives up ownership without destroying the object.

12
00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:08.440
If release is used, a new owner should take responsibility immediately.

13
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:14.400
unique_ptr can also carry a custom deleter for resources that are not released with ordinary delete .

14
00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:19.700
That makes it useful at library boundaries, but the deleter must match the creation API.

15
00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:28.120
Overall, exclusive ownership keeps object graphs smaller, cleanup paths easier to test, and lifetime responsibilities easier to maintain.
